.TH HIRO-UI 1 "August 2026" "HIRO" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hiro-ui \- desktop-agnostic status indicator and approval prompt for HIRO
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hiro-ui
[\fB\-\-socket\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
HIRO ("Hello, InfraRed, On Linux") provides Windows Hello-style face
authentication on Linux using the built-in IR camera. \fBhiro-ui\fR is the
desktop-agnostic session UI: a small, frameless, always-on-top card centered
on the primary monitor that shows scanning progress and
liveness meters while your face is authenticated, Allow/Deny prompts when a
non-login request (sudo, polkit, lock screen, ...) needs an explicit
decision, and a result flash with the verdict.
.PP
It is a per-user, per-session process started by the systemd user service
\fBhiro-ui.service\fR (or the XDG autostart entry), and it consumes the same
state stream as the GNOME Shell extension \fBhiro-status@hiro\fR. It renders
nothing while that extension reports enabled (the extension owns the UI
there; detection probes the running shell over D-Bus, so it does not depend
on desktop environment variables); see the \fI[ui]\fR section of
\fBhiro.conf\fR(5) to override detection. On the lock screen or login
greeter an in-session overlay cannot be shown; those requests fall back to
your password.
.PP
The daemon must be running for the UI to display anything. The card hides
while the daemon is unreachable and reappears on reconnect.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --socket PATH
Daemon socket path (default \fI/run/hirod/hirod.sock\fR).
.TP
.B --config PATH
Configuration file (default \fI/etc/hiro/config.toml\fR). Missing or
unreadable files fall back to the defaults.
.TP
.B --version
Print version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/hirod/hirod.sock
Daemon socket.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hiro (1),
.BR hirod (8),
.BR hiro.conf (5)
